{"author":{"id":"8d2e0405f08ac6c481f912544d6d5c0f58b2dc14a7e51ad4d499ee7a34567943"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1786797049,"nonce":"PHR4W8lzYecxAUYxSxK/bxVxs4U=","metadata":{"github-id":"I_kwDORPa0Bs8AAAABM4Wp1g","github-url":"https://github.com/FhG-IISB/jNO/issues/100","origin":"github"},"title":"crux.eval of a parametric fem.solve ignores in-place parameter module mutation","message":"## What\n\nEvaluating a parametric `fem.solve()` node via `crux.eval` after mutating the parameter's stored module in place (`eqx.tree_at`, the documented data-field idiom from `tests/test_domain_decomposition.py`) returns the value at the **old** parameter — silently.\n\n## Reproduction (isolated 2026-08-15)\n\n```python\ng = jno.np.parameter((1,), name=\"g\", key=jax.random.PRNGKey(0))\nfem = jno.fem([ui.x*vi.x + ui.y*vi.y - 1.0*vi, u(xb, yb) - g])   # parametric (Dirichlet value)\ncrux = jno.core([(fem.solve()).mse], domain=d)\n\ndef at(val):\n    g.model.module = eqx.tree_at(lambda m: m.value, g.model.module, jnp.asarray([val]))\n    return float(np.asarray(crux.eval([fem.solve()])).reshape(-1).mean())\n\nat(1.0)   # 0.0220\nat(5.0)   # 0.0220  \u003c-- identical to the last bit; the mutation was ignored\n```\n\nTraining through `crux.solve` works (the same session's boundary-value recovery tests hit \u003c1% on three solve paths), so the staleness is specific to the **eval-after-mutation** path — either an eval-side cache keyed on the expression, or a captured module snapshot.\n\n## Why it matters\n\nThis is the silent-wrong class: a user sweeping a parameter by mutation + eval gets a flat response and no error. It also cost a debugging round in this session (a gradient test read slope = 0.000 and looked like a broken feature).\n\nEither honor the live module at eval time, or refuse/warn when a registered parameter's module has changed since compile. A test pinning whichever contract is chosen belongs next to the DD data-field tests.","files":null}]}